        "prompt": {
          "raw": "You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\nCan I just set a field on currentState() inside my KV entity handler and return done?",
          "label": "evals/akka-sdk-key-value-entities/prompt-baseline.txt: You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\n{{request}}",
          "config": {}
        },
        "promptId": "3e304803a50c8734927e8af6990274b49b4247cea2e2e209cea68d65819ead49",
        "promptIdx": 0,
        "provider": {
          "id": "exec:../_provider/claude.sh",
          "label": "sonnet-baseline"
        },
        "response": {
          "output": "No. Mutating `currentState()` doesn't persist anything. The runtime only writes state when the handler returns an effect that carries it, so the change is lost when the command ends.\n\nBuild a new state and return it through the effect:\n\n```java\npublic Effect<Done> setName(String name) {\n  var updated = currentState().withName(name);   // records: copy, don't mutate\n  return effects().updateState(updated).thenReply(Done.getInstance());\n}\n```\n\n`effects().reply(Done.getInstance())` on its own is a read-only reply and leaves the stored state untouched.\n\nState should be an immutable record, so a field setter usually won't compile anyway. On the first command for a new entity, `currentState()` returns `emptyState()`, which is `null` by default. Override `emptyState()` if you want a non-null starting value."
        },
        "score": 1,
        "success": true,
        "testCase": {
          "description": "mutate",
          "vars": {
            "request": "file:///private/tmp/b6/evals/akka-sdk-key-value-entities/cases/mutate.txt"
